# Every text file is LF in every checkout, on every OS, whatever core.autocrlf
# says. Without this a Windows checkout holds CRLF: gofmt -l then reports files
# that are formatted, a regeneration check reports every generated file as
# changed, and a shell script or package maintainer script that Linux reads
# fails on its carriage returns. Files git decides are binary are left alone
# by text=auto; the list below says so outright for the kinds this project
# has, so that one that git mistakes for text is never rewritten.
#
# A fixture whose bytes must stay exactly as committed (raw CRLF on purpose)
# is marked with its own `path -text` line below; none is needed today.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.tar binary
*.xz binary
*.pdf binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.syso binary

# Files a Linux package installs or runs are read by Linux tools that treat a
# carriage return as part of the file: a #!/bin/sh\r script does not run, and
# desktop-file-validate rejects a desktop entry. A Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf would otherwise build a .deb or .rpm that fails to install.
*.sh text eol=lf
build/linux/* text eol=lf
cmd/release/nfpm.yaml text eol=lf
